Edition No. 36 — Friday, 6 November 2026

10 stories: 'An imaginary issue': the government asks civil society to rethink its 25 November term-limits march, as the Evangelical Fellowship demands that the debate end now (lead); Back to Lusaka: Judge Musona moves the Mundubile–Zulu sedition case out of Solwezi, while the treason bail hearing for 18 accused is put back to Friday 6 November; Mandevu has an MP without a ballot: Adrian Banda is declared elected, a minister denies pushing out the Tonse candidate, and the ECZ says it counts votes, not makes them; 'We don't arrest on rumours': the ACC says it is investigating seven Auditor General queries and six FIC referrals, as suspected illicit flows fall to US$1.8 billion; Fourteen thousand stalls, 405 allocated: Sialubalo tells councils to move street vendors with dignity and hand out market spaces fairly, a month into Operation Clear Path; Paid in advance, never delivered: the ZPPA bars two suppliers of school laboratory and home economics equipment from public tenders for a year over World Bank-funded contracts; US$240 million of US$1.52 billion: the Rural Electrification Authority tells the Livingstone summit how far it has got with the master plan for universal access by 2030; Five from five: Munali Suns finish Group E unbeaten with an 80–60 win over Soweto Panthers and head for the Elite 16 in Nairobi from 23 November; Third year on the list: Barbra Banda is shortlisted for the FIFPRO Women's World 11 again, with the final team to be announced on Monday 9 November; Nkana go top of Stream A, ten-man Zanaco throw away a lead against MUZA, and Nkwazi end Red Arrows' 19-month unbeaten home run before the international break.


